(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("storage",{"storage/block-file-and-object-storage":{"html":"\u003cp>Everything an application saves ends up on some kind of storage. The kind decides what you can do with it, how fast it is, and what breaks when it fills up.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Block, file and object storage and when to use each\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>IOPS, throughput and latency, and how they differ\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The main device types: HDD, SSD and NVMe\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to measure a disk from the command line\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"three-kinds\">Three kinds\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Kind\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>You see\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Examples\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Good for\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Block\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A raw disk you format yourself\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Local disk, SAN LUN, AWS EBS, Azure managed disk\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Databases, boot disks, virtual machines\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>File\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Folders and files shared over a network\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>NFS, SMB, AWS EFS, Azure Files\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Shared content, home folders\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Object\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Whole objects with keys, over HTTP\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>AWS S3, Azure Blob, Ceph RGW\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Backups, media, logs, data lakes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A block device has no idea what is in it. A \u003cstrong>filesystem\u003c/strong> on top gives it names and folders. Object storage has no folders, only keys that may contain slashes, and objects are replaced as a whole, not edited in place.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-numbers\">The numbers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Capacity\u003c/strong>: how much it holds.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>IOPS\u003c/strong>: input and output operations per second. Many small reads and writes, such as a database, are IOPS limited.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Throughput\u003c/strong>: megabytes per second. Large sequential copies, such as backups, are throughput limited.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Latency\u003c/strong>: the time for one operation. A database commit waits for it.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>A disk that does 100 000 IOPS with 4 KB operations moves only about 400 MB per second. The same disk copying large blocks may move 2 000 MB per second. Always ask which number a workload needs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"device-types\">Device types\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>HDD\u003c/strong> (spinning): cheap per terabyte, a few hundred IOPS, milliseconds of latency.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>SSD (SATA or SAS)\u003c/strong>: tens of thousands of IOPS, latency well under a millisecond.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>NVMe\u003c/strong>: flash on the PCIe bus, hundreds of thousands of IOPS and very low latency.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Cloud disks add limits of their own: a volume may have a baseline and a burst. When the burst credit is spent, it slows to the baseline.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measuring\">Measuring\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"lsblk\ndf -h /\nfio --name=randread --rw=randread --bs=4k --size=1G --runtime=10 --time_based --iodepth=16\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>lsblk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME   MAJ:MIN RM  SIZE RO TYPE MOUNTPOINTS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">sda      8:0    0  100G  0 disk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">|-sda1   8:1    0    1G  0 part /boot\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">`-sda2   8:2    0   99G  0 part /\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span> -h /\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Filesystem      Size  Used Avail Use% Mounted on\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/dev/sda2        98G   71G   22G  77% /\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>fio --name=randread --rw=randread --bs=4k --size=1G --runtime=10 --time_based --iodepth=16\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  read: IOPS=21.4k, BW=83.7MiB/s\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>fio\u003c/code> is the standard tool for testing, and it should be run on a test disk, never on a disk with live data in a way that writes over files you need.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-cheap-disk-that-made-the-database-slow\">Incident: The cheap disk that made the database slow\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A report job that took ten minutes on the old server took two hours after migration to a cheaper volume type.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"iostat -x 5 2 | grep -E 'Device|nvme|sda'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>iostat -x 5 2 | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Device|nvme|sda&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Device   r/s    w/s   rkB/s  await  %util\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">sda      95.0   12.0  1520   42.10  99.80\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The new volume was a throughput-oriented HDD type. The database made many small random reads, so the disk was at 99.8 percent busy and each read waited 42 ms. The old disk was SSD. Moving the data to an SSD volume brought the report back to ten minutes. Capacity and price were not the issue; the access pattern was.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choosing a volume by price per terabyte without checking IOPS\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating object storage like a filesystem that supports small in-place edits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Testing with fio on a path that has a cache and getting numbers that are too good\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring cloud burst limits until a busy hour\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"three-kinds","text":"Three kinds"},{"id":"the-numbers","text":"The numbers"},{"id":"device-types","text":"Device types"},{"id":"measuring","text":"Measuring"},{"id":"incident-the-cheap-disk-that-made-the-database-slow","text":"Incident: The cheap disk that made the database slow"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Linux kernel: block layer","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/block/index.html"},{"t":"fio documentation","u":"https://fio.readthedocs.io/en/latest/"},{"t":"AWS: EBS volume types","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ebs/latest/userguide/ebs-volume-types.html"}],"text":"Everything an application saves ends up on some kind of storage. The kind decides what you can do with it, how fast it is, and what breaks when it fills up. What you will learn Block, file and object storage and when to use each IOPS, throughput and latency, and how they differ The main device types: HDD, SSD and NVMe How to measure a disk from the command line Three kinds Kind You see Examples Good for Block A raw disk you format yourself Local disk, SAN LUN, AWS EBS, Azure managed disk Databases, boot disks, virtual machines File Folders and files shared over a network NFS, SMB, AWS EFS, Azure Files Shared content, home folders Object Whole objects with keys, over HTTP AWS S3, Azure Blob, Ceph RGW Backups, media, logs, data lakes A block device has no idea what is in it. A filesystem on top gives it names and folders. Object storage has no folders, only keys that may contain slashes, and objects are replaced as a whole, not edited in place. The numbers Capacity : how much it holds. IOPS : input and output operations per second. Many small reads and writes, such as a database, are IOPS limited. Throughput : megabytes per second. Large sequential copies, such as backups, are throughput limited. Latency : the time for one operation. A database commit waits for it. A disk that does 100 000 IOPS with 4 KB operations moves only about 400 MB per second. The same disk copying large blocks may move 2 000 MB per second. Always ask which number a workload needs. Device types HDD (spinning): cheap per terabyte, a few hundred IOPS, milliseconds of latency. SSD (SATA or SAS) : tens of thousands of IOPS, latency well under a millisecond. NVMe : flash on the PCIe bus, hundreds of thousands of IOPS and very low latency. Cloud disks add limits of their own: a volume may have a baseline and a burst. When the burst credit is spent, it slows to the baseline. Measuring $ lsblk NAME MAJ:MIN RM SIZE RO TYPE MOUNTPOINTS sda 8:0 0 100G 0 disk |-sda1 8:1 0 1G 0 part /boot `-sda2 8:2 0 99G 0 part / $ df -h / Filesystem Size Used Avail Use% Mounted on /dev/sda2 98G 71G 22G 77% / $ fio --name=randread --rw=randread --bs=4k --size=1G --runtime=10 --time_based --iodepth=16 read: IOPS=21.4k, BW=83.7MiB/s fio is the standard tool for testing, and it should be run on a test disk, never on a disk with live data in a way that writes over files you need. Incident: The cheap disk that made the database slow Incident A report job that took ten minutes on the old server took two hours after migration to a cheaper volume type. $ iostat -x 5 2 | grep -E &#x27;Device|nvme|sda&#x27; Device r/s w/s rkB/s await %util sda 95.0 12.0 1520 42.10 99.80 The new volume was a throughput-oriented HDD type. The database made many small random reads, so the disk was at 99.8 percent busy and each read waited 42 ms. The old disk was SSD. Moving the data to an SSD volume brought the report back to ten minutes. Capacity and price were not the issue; the access pattern was. Common mistakes Choosing a volume by price per terabyte without checking IOPS Treating object storage like a filesystem that supports small in-place edits Testing with fio on a path that has a cache and getting numbers that are too good Ignoring cloud burst limits until a busy hour"},"storage/filesystems-ext4-xfs-and-mounts":{"html":"\u003cp>A filesystem turns a block device into folders and files. Picking one is a smaller choice than most people expect, but mounting it correctly matters a great deal.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>ext4, XFS, btrfs and ZFS in a few sentences each\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Inodes, journals and why a disk can be full with space free\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mounting by UUID and writing a safe fstab line\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What to do when a filesystem needs repair\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-filesystems\">Common filesystems\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>ext4\u003c/strong>: the long-time Linux default. Stable, well understood, can grow and, with care, shrink.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>XFS\u003c/strong>: strong with large files and many parallel writes, the default on Red Hat family systems. It can grow while mounted but cannot shrink.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>btrfs\u003c/strong>: copy-on-write with snapshots and checksums, used by some distributions.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>ZFS\u003c/strong>: combines volume management and filesystem, with checksums, snapshots and replication. Its licence keeps it outside the main Linux kernel.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Pick what your distribution supports and your team knows. Most production trouble comes from full disks and bad mounts, not from the choice between ext4 and XFS.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"inodes-and-journals\">Inodes and journals\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>inode\u003c/strong> holds a file&#39;s metadata: owner, permissions, size and where its data blocks are. A filesystem has a fixed number of inodes on ext4. A disk with millions of tiny files can run out of inodes while plenty of bytes are free, and every write fails with &quot;No space left on device&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"df -h /var\ndf -i /var\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span> -h /var\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Filesystem      Size  Used Avail Use% Mounted on\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/dev/sdb1       200G   61G  129G  32% /var\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span> -i /var\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Filesystem      Inodes   IUsed IFree IUse% Mounted on\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/dev/sdb1     13107200 13107200     0  100% /var\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>journal\u003c/strong> records changes before making them, so after a crash the filesystem replays the journal instead of checking everything.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"mounting\">Mounting\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"blkid /dev/sdb1\nsudo mkdir -p /data\nsudo mount /dev/sdb1 /data\nfindmnt /data\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>blkid /dev/sdb1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/dev/sdb1: UUID=&quot;3f1c0d7e-8a42-4b9b-9c11-2e6a5d0f1a77&quot; TYPE=&quot;ext4&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">mkdir\u003c/span> -p /data\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> mount /dev/sdb1 /data\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>findmnt /data\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">TARGET SOURCE    FSTYPE OPTIONS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/data  /dev/sdb1 ext4   rw,relatime\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Device names such as \u003ccode>/dev/sdb1\u003c/code> can change between boots. In \u003ccode>/etc/fstab\u003c/code>, refer to the disk by \u003cstrong>UUID\u003c/strong>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"UUID=3f1c0d7e-8a42-4b9b-9c11-2e6a5d0f1a77  /data  ext4  defaults,nofail  0  2\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">UUID=3f1c0d7e-8a42-4b9b-9c11-2e6a5d0f1a77  /data  ext4  defaults,nofail  0  2\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The six fields are device, mount point, type, options, dump (leave 0) and fsck order. \u003ccode>nofail\u003c/code> lets the server boot even if the disk is missing. Without it, a missing data disk can drop the machine into emergency mode. After editing fstab, always run \u003ccode>sudo mount -a\u003c/code>: it mounts everything in the file and shows an error now, not at the next reboot.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"repair\">Repair\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An unclean shutdown usually needs only a journal replay. When \u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> is needed, run it on an unmounted filesystem and read what it will change first. Never run it on a mounted disk. For a disk with hardware errors, copy what you can with a tool such as \u003ccode>ddrescue\u003c/code> before repairing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-server-that-did-not-come-back\">Incident: The server that did not come back\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a routine reboot a server stayed in emergency mode and the team had no console access plan.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[ TIME ] Timed out waiting for device /dev/disk/by-uuid/3f1c0d7e-8a42-4b9b-9c11-2e6a5d0f1a7.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[DEPEND] Dependency failed for /data.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">You are in emergency mode.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Someone had retyped a UUID into fstab and dropped the last character, and the line had no \u003ccode>nofail\u003c/code>. The system waited for a device that did not exist. Fixing the UUID in \u003ccode>/etc/fstab\u003c/code> from the emergency shell and rebooting restored service. The team now copies UUIDs from \u003ccode>blkid\u003c/code>, adds \u003ccode>nofail\u003c/code> for data disks, and runs \u003ccode>mount -a\u003c/code> before leaving the server.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> Mount a new disk by its device name in fstab.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>/dev/sdb1  /data  ext4  defaults  0 2\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>(works)\n# after adding another disk, names shift\nmount: /data: wrong fs type, bad option, bad superblock on /dev/sdb1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> Device names depend on detection order and can change at reboot. The wrong disk may be mounted or the boot may stop.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>blkid /dev/sdb1\nUUID=7f1c2a9e-0d3a-4c1e-9f55-1d6b2a0a77c1  /data  ext4  defaults,nofail  0 2\nmount -a\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Use the UUID, add nofail for non-critical mounts, and run mount -a before rebooting.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Referring to disks by \u003ccode>/dev/sdX\u003c/code> in fstab\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing fstab and not testing with \u003ccode>mount -a\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Looking only at \u003ccode>df -h\u003c/code> and forgetting \u003ccode>df -i\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running fsck on a mounted filesystem\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"common-filesystems","text":"Common filesystems"},{"id":"inodes-and-journals","text":"Inodes and journals"},{"id":"mounting","text":"Mounting"},{"id":"repair","text":"Repair"},{"id":"incident-the-server-that-did-not-come-back","text":"Incident: The server that did not come back"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"ext4 documentation","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/ext4.html"},{"t":"XFS documentation","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/filesystems/xfs/index.html"},{"t":"fstab manual page","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/fstab.5.html"}],"text":"A filesystem turns a block device into folders and files. Picking one is a smaller choice than most people expect, but mounting it correctly matters a great deal. What you will learn ext4, XFS, btrfs and ZFS in a few sentences each Inodes, journals and why a disk can be full with space free Mounting by UUID and writing a safe fstab line What to do when a filesystem needs repair Common filesystems ext4 : the long-time Linux default. Stable, well understood, can grow and, with care, shrink. XFS : strong with large files and many parallel writes, the default on Red Hat family systems. It can grow while mounted but cannot shrink. btrfs : copy-on-write with snapshots and checksums, used by some distributions. ZFS : combines volume management and filesystem, with checksums, snapshots and replication. Its licence keeps it outside the main Linux kernel. Pick what your distribution supports and your team knows. Most production trouble comes from full disks and bad mounts, not from the choice between ext4 and XFS. Inodes and journals An inode holds a file&#39;s metadata: owner, permissions, size and where its data blocks are. A filesystem has a fixed number of inodes on ext4. A disk with millions of tiny files can run out of inodes while plenty of bytes are free, and every write fails with \"No space left on device\". $ df -h /var Filesystem Size Used Avail Use% Mounted on /dev/sdb1 200G 61G 129G 32% /var $ df -i /var Filesystem Inodes IUsed IFree IUse% Mounted on /dev/sdb1 13107200 13107200 0 100% /var A journal records changes before making them, so after a crash the filesystem replays the journal instead of checking everything. Mounting $ blkid /dev/sdb1 /dev/sdb1: UUID=\"3f1c0d7e-8a42-4b9b-9c11-2e6a5d0f1a77\" TYPE=\"ext4\" $ sudo mkdir -p /data $ sudo mount /dev/sdb1 /data $ findmnt /data TARGET SOURCE FSTYPE OPTIONS /data /dev/sdb1 ext4 rw,relatime Device names such as /dev/sdb1 can change between boots. In /etc/fstab , refer to the disk by UUID : UUID=3f1c0d7e-8a42-4b9b-9c11-2e6a5d0f1a77 /data ext4 defaults,nofail 0 2 The six fields are device, mount point, type, options, dump (leave 0) and fsck order. nofail lets the server boot even if the disk is missing. Without it, a missing data disk can drop the machine into emergency mode. After editing fstab, always run sudo mount -a : it mounts everything in the file and shows an error now, not at the next reboot. Repair An unclean shutdown usually needs only a journal replay. When fsck is needed, run it on an unmounted filesystem and read what it will change first. Never run it on a mounted disk. For a disk with hardware errors, copy what you can with a tool such as ddrescue before repairing. Incident: The server that did not come back Incident After a routine reboot a server stayed in emergency mode and the team had no console access plan. [ TIME ] Timed out waiting for device /dev/disk/by-uuid/3f1c0d7e-8a42-4b9b-9c11-2e6a5d0f1a7. [DEPEND] Dependency failed for /data. You are in emergency mode. Someone had retyped a UUID into fstab and dropped the last character, and the line had no nofail . The system waited for a device that did not exist. Fixing the UUID in /etc/fstab from the emergency shell and rebooting restored service. The team now copies UUIDs from blkid , adds nofail for data disks, and runs mount -a before leaving the server. Worked example: a tempting wrong path Goal. Mount a new disk by its device name in fstab. The tempting way /dev/sdb1 /data ext4 defaults 0 2 See what goes wrong (works) # after adding another disk, names shift mount: /data: wrong fs type, bad option, bad superblock on /dev/sdb1 Why it fails. Device names depend on detection order and can change at reboot. The wrong disk may be mounted or the boot may stop. See the way that works The way that works blkid /dev/sdb1 UUID=7f1c2a9e-0d3a-4c1e-9f55-1d6b2a0a77c1 /data ext4 defaults,nofail 0 2 mount -a Use the UUID, add nofail for non-critical mounts, and run mount -a before rebooting. Common mistakes Referring to dis"},"storage/lvm-and-raid":{"html":"\u003cp>A server&#39;s data will outgrow its disk. LVM lets you add space without moving data, and RAID lets a disk fail without losing it. They solve different problems and are often used together.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Physical volumes, volume groups and logical volumes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Growing a volume and its filesystem online\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>RAID 0, 1, 5, 6 and 10 and when each fits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What to do when a disk fails\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"lvm\">LVM\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>LVM\u003c/strong> (logical volume manager) adds a layer between disks and filesystems.\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>physical volume (PV)\u003c/strong> is a disk or partition given to LVM.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>volume group (VG)\u003c/strong> is a pool made of one or more PVs.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>logical volume (LV)\u003c/strong> is a slice of the pool that you format and mount.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo pvs\nsudo vgs\nsudo lvs\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> pvs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  PV         VG   Fmt  Attr PSize   PFree\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  /dev/sdb   vg0  lvm2 a--  500.00g 120.00g\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> vgs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  VG   #PV #LV #SN Attr   VSize   VFree\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  vg0    1   2   0 wz--n- 500.00g 120.00g\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> lvs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  LV   VG  Attr       LSize\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  data vg0 -wi-ao---- 300.00g\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  logs vg0 -wi-ao---- 80.00g\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>To grow the \u003ccode>data\u003c/code> volume by 50 GB, the pool needs free space (here 120 GB is free) and the filesystem must grow too:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo lvextend -r -L +50G /dev/vg0/data\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> lvextend -r -L +50G /dev/vg0/data\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  Size of logical volume vg0/data changed from 300.00 GiB to 350.00 GiB.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  Logical volume vg0/data successfully resized.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>-r\u003c/code> option resizes the filesystem as well. Without it the volume is larger but \u003ccode>df\u003c/code> shows the old size, because the filesystem still thinks it has 300 GiB. In that case run \u003ccode>resize2fs\u003c/code> (ext4) or \u003ccode>xfs_growfs\u003c/code> (XFS). If the pool has no free space, add a new disk with \u003ccode>pvcreate\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>vgextend\u003c/code> first. XFS can grow but not shrink.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"raid\">RAID\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>RAID\u003c/strong> combines disks for speed, safety or both. It protects against disk failure, not against deletion, corruption or fire. It is not a backup.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Level\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Disks\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Survives\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Notes\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>2+\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Nothing\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Speed only: one failure loses everything\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>2\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>One disk\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Mirror, half the capacity\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>5\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>3+\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>One disk\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Parity, slow to rebuild on big disks\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>6\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>4+\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Two disks\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Double parity, better for large disks\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>10\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>4+\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>One per mirror\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Mirrors striped, fast and safe\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Rebuilding a large disk takes many hours, and the surviving disks work hard the whole time. With RAID 5 on big disks the chance of a second failure during rebuild is real, which is why RAID 6 or RAID 10 is preferred for large arrays.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cat /proc/mdstat\nsudo mdadm --detail /dev/md0 | grep -E 'State|Failed'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /proc/mdstat\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">md0 : active raid1 sdb1[0] sdc1[1](F)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      976630336 blocks [2/1] [U_]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> mdadm --detail /dev/md0 | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;State|Failed&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">          State : clean, degraded\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> Failed Devices : 1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>[U_]\u003c/code> shows one of two members is missing: the array is \u003cstrong>degraded\u003c/strong> but working.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-two-failures-one-night\">Incident: Two failures, one night\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A RAID 5 array lost a second disk while rebuilding the first.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cat /proc/mdstat\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /proc/mdstat\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">md0 : active raid5 sde1[4](S) sdd1[3](F) sdc1[2] sdb1[1](F) sda1[0]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      [5/3] [U_U__]\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>One disk had failed a week before, the alert went to an unmonitored mailbox, and the rebuild began only after a second disk showed errors. With two members gone the array stopped. The team restored from last night&#39;s backup and rebuilt the array as RAID 6 with working alerts. RAID kept the first failure harmless; nothing in the design covered a second one that nobody knew about.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> RAID 5 on large disks is cheap redundancy, so use it everywhere.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>mdadm --create /dev/md0 --level=5 --raid-devices=6 /dev/sd[b-g]1  # 16 TB disks\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>(works)\n# a disk fails, rebuild takes 3 days\nsecond disk reports read errors during rebuild\nmd0: array failed\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> With very large disks the rebuild takes days, and during that time a second failure or an unreadable sector loses the array. Redundancy is only as good as the rebuild window.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>mdadm --create /dev/md0 --level=6 --raid-devices=6 /dev/sd[b-g]1\n# and tested backups\nmdadm --detail /dev/md0\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Use RAID 6 or mirrors for big disks, monitor rebuilds, and keep backups because RAID is not a backup.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating RAID as a backup\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Extending an LV and forgetting to grow the filesystem\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No alert on a degraded array\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>RAID 5 on very large disks with no spare\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"lvm","text":"LVM"},{"id":"raid","text":"RAID"},{"id":"incident-two-failures-one-night","text":"Incident: Two failures, one night"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"LVM2 resource page","u":"https://sourceware.org/lvm2/"},{"t":"Linux RAID wiki and mdadm","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/md.html"},{"t":"Red Hat: configuring LVM","u":"https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html/configuring_and_managing_logical_volumes/index"}],"text":"A server&#39;s data will outgrow its disk. LVM lets you add space without moving data, and RAID lets a disk fail without losing it. They solve different problems and are often used together. What you will learn Physical volumes, volume groups and logical volumes Growing a volume and its filesystem online RAID 0, 1, 5, 6 and 10 and when each fits What to do when a disk fails LVM LVM (logical volume manager) adds a layer between disks and filesystems. A physical volume (PV) is a disk or partition given to LVM. A volume group (VG) is a pool made of one or more PVs. A logical volume (LV) is a slice of the pool that you format and mount. $ sudo pvs PV VG Fmt Attr PSize PFree /dev/sdb vg0 lvm2 a-- 500.00g 120.00g $ sudo vgs VG #PV #LV #SN Attr VSize VFree vg0 1 2 0 wz--n- 500.00g 120.00g $ sudo lvs LV VG Attr LSize data vg0 -wi-ao---- 300.00g logs vg0 -wi-ao---- 80.00g To grow the data volume by 50 GB, the pool needs free space (here 120 GB is free) and the filesystem must grow too: $ sudo lvextend -r -L +50G /dev/vg0/data Size of logical volume vg0/data changed from 300.00 GiB to 350.00 GiB. Logical volume vg0/data successfully resized. The -r option resizes the filesystem as well. Without it the volume is larger but df shows the old size, because the filesystem still thinks it has 300 GiB. In that case run resize2fs (ext4) or xfs_growfs (XFS). If the pool has no free space, add a new disk with pvcreate and vgextend first. XFS can grow but not shrink. RAID RAID combines disks for speed, safety or both. It protects against disk failure, not against deletion, corruption or fire. It is not a backup. Level Disks Survives Notes 0 2+ Nothing Speed only: one failure loses everything 1 2 One disk Mirror, half the capacity 5 3+ One disk Parity, slow to rebuild on big disks 6 4+ Two disks Double parity, better for large disks 10 4+ One per mirror Mirrors striped, fast and safe Rebuilding a large disk takes many hours, and the surviving disks work hard the whole time. With RAID 5 on big disks the chance of a second failure during rebuild is real, which is why RAID 6 or RAID 10 is preferred for large arrays. $ cat /proc/mdstat md0 : active raid1 sdb1[0] sdc1[1](F) 976630336 blocks [2/1] [U_] $ sudo mdadm --detail /dev/md0 | grep -E &#x27;State|Failed&#x27; State : clean, degraded Failed Devices : 1 [U_] shows one of two members is missing: the array is degraded but working. Incident: Two failures, one night Incident A RAID 5 array lost a second disk while rebuilding the first. $ cat /proc/mdstat md0 : active raid5 sde1[4](S) sdd1[3](F) sdc1[2] sdb1[1](F) sda1[0] [5/3] [U_U__] One disk had failed a week before, the alert went to an unmonitored mailbox, and the rebuild began only after a second disk showed errors. With two members gone the array stopped. The team restored from last night&#39;s backup and rebuilt the array as RAID 6 with working alerts. RAID kept the first failure harmless; nothing in the design covered a second one that nobody knew about. Worked example: a tempting wrong path Goal. RAID 5 on large disks is cheap redundancy, so use it everywhere. The tempting way mdadm --create /dev/md0 --level=5 --raid-devices=6 /dev/sd[b-g]1 # 16 TB disks See what goes wrong (works) # a disk fails, rebuild takes 3 days second disk reports read errors during rebuild md0: array failed Why it fails. With very large disks the rebuild takes days, and during that time a second failure or an unreadable sector loses the array. Redundancy is only as good as the rebuild window. See the way that works The way that works mdadm --create /dev/md0 --level=6 --raid-devices=6 /dev/sd[b-g]1 # and tested backups mdadm --detail /dev/md0 Use RAID 6 or mirrors for big disks, monitor rebuilds, and keep backups because RAID is not a backup. Common mistakes Treating RAID as a backup Extending an LV and forgetting to grow the filesystem No alert on a degraded array RAID 5 on very large disks with no spare"},"storage/network-storage-nfs-iscsi-and-ceph":{"html":"\u003cp>When many servers need the same data, or a server&#39;s disk must outlive the server, storage moves onto the network.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>NAS and SAN, and how NFS and iSCSI fit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mounting NFS and the options that matter\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>iSCSI initiators and targets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What Ceph is and how it keeps copies\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"nas-and-san\">NAS and SAN\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>NAS\u003c/strong> (network attached storage) shares \u003cstrong>files\u003c/strong>. Clients see folders. NFS and SMB are NAS protocols.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>SAN\u003c/strong> (storage area network) shares \u003cstrong>blocks\u003c/strong>. Clients see a disk and format it. iSCSI and Fibre Channel are SAN protocols.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"nfs\">NFS\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>NFS lets a server export a folder that clients mount like a local one.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"showmount -e nas01\nsudo mount -t nfs4 -o rw,hard,timeo=600 nas01:/exports/media /mnt/media\nfindmnt /mnt/media\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>showmount -e nas01\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Export list for nas01:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/exports/media 10.0.0.0/24\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> mount -t nfs4 -o rw,hard,timeo=600 nas01:/exports/media /mnt/media\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>findmnt /mnt/media\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">TARGET     SOURCE            FSTYPE OPTIONS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/mnt/media nas01:/exports/media nfs4  rw,relatime,hard,timeo=600\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Options worth knowing:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>hard\u003c/strong> keeps retrying when the server is unreachable, so applications wait instead of getting errors that corrupt data. \u003cstrong>soft\u003c/strong> gives up and returns an error, which can silently lose writes.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>File ownership uses numbers (UID and GID). If the user is 1001 on the client and 1002 on the server, permissions look wrong. Either keep IDs in sync or use a central directory.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A client that cannot reach the server may freeze commands such as \u003ccode>ls\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>df\u003c/code> on that mount. Applications that hang in uninterruptible sleep (state \u003ccode>D\u003c/code>) are a common sign.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"iscsi\">iSCSI\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>iSCSI carries SCSI commands over ordinary IP. The server that offers a disk is the \u003cstrong>target\u003c/strong>, and the client is the \u003cstrong>initiator\u003c/strong>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo iscsiadm -m discovery -t sendtargets -p 10.0.1.20\nsudo iscsiadm -m node -T iqn.2026-01.example:storage.lun1 -p 10.0.1.20 --login\nlsblk | grep sdc\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> iscsiadm -m discovery -t sendtargets -p 10.0.1.20\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">10.0.1.20:3260,1 iqn.2026-01.example:storage.lun1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> iscsiadm -m node -T iqn.2026-01.example:storage.lun1 -p 10.0.1.20 --login\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>lsblk | grep sdc\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">sdc      8:32   0  200G  0 disk\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The new disk is raw; you create a filesystem on it. Use a separate network or VLAN for iSCSI, and \u003cstrong>multipath\u003c/strong> (two paths to the storage) so one switch failure does not remove the disk.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ceph\">Ceph\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Ceph\u003c/strong> is a distributed storage system that runs on ordinary servers. It offers block (RBD), file (CephFS) and object (RGW, S3 compatible) storage on the same cluster.\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>OSDs\u003c/strong> store the data, one per disk.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Monitors\u003c/strong> keep the cluster map and need a majority to agree.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Data is split into objects placed by an algorithm called \u003cstrong>CRUSH\u003c/strong>, so no central table is needed.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Each object has copies on different hosts (commonly 3), or is spread with \u003cstrong>erasure coding\u003c/strong>, which uses less space but costs CPU and repair work.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo ceph -s\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> ceph -s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  cluster:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    health: HEALTH_WARN\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">            1 osds down\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  data:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    pools:   3 pools, 128 pgs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    usage:   4.1 TiB used, 18 TiB / 22 TiB avail\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    pgs:     128 active+undersized+degraded\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A down OSD leaves data with fewer copies than intended, shown as \u003cstrong>degraded\u003c/strong>. Ceph rebuilds copies on other disks automatically. Replacing the disk quickly keeps the cluster safe, and a full cluster is a serious failure: Ceph stops writes at about 95 percent full to protect itself.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-share-that-froze-every-server\">Incident: The share that froze every server\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>One NFS server was rebooted, and thirty application servers hung within a minute.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ps -eo pid,stat,wchan:20,cmd | grep ' D '\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ps -eo pid,\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">stat\u003c/span>,wchan:20,cmd | grep \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27; D &#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2231 D    nfs_wait_bit_killable  /opt/app/report-worker\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2240 D    nfs_wait_bit_killable  df -h\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The applications were mounted with \u003ccode>hard\u003c/code>, so they waited for the server, which is the safe choice for data but blocks everything. Because even \u003ccode>df -h\u003c/code> touched the mount, monitoring checks hung too. The NFS server came back after 12 minutes and everything resumed. The fix was to make the exports highly available, and to stop monitoring scripts calling \u003ccode>df\u003c/code> on network mounts without a timeout.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using soft mounts for data that matters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Different user IDs on client and server\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>iSCSI on the same network as general traffic, with no multipath\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Letting a Ceph cluster fill past 85 percent\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"nas-and-san","text":"NAS and SAN"},{"id":"nfs","text":"NFS"},{"id":"iscsi","text":"iSCSI"},{"id":"ceph","text":"Ceph"},{"id":"incident-the-share-that-froze-every-server","text":"Incident: The share that froze every server"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Linux NFS documentation","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/nfs/index.html"},{"t":"Ceph documentation","u":"https://docs.ceph.com/en/latest/"},{"t":"Red Hat: managing storage devices","u":"https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html/managing_storage_devices/index"}],"text":"When many servers need the same data, or a server&#39;s disk must outlive the server, storage moves onto the network. What you will learn NAS and SAN, and how NFS and iSCSI fit Mounting NFS and the options that matter iSCSI initiators and targets What Ceph is and how it keeps copies NAS and SAN NAS (network attached storage) shares files . Clients see folders. NFS and SMB are NAS protocols. SAN (storage area network) shares blocks . Clients see a disk and format it. iSCSI and Fibre Channel are SAN protocols. NFS NFS lets a server export a folder that clients mount like a local one. $ showmount -e nas01 Export list for nas01: /exports/media 10.0.0.0/24 $ sudo mount -t nfs4 -o rw,hard,timeo=600 nas01:/exports/media /mnt/media $ findmnt /mnt/media TARGET SOURCE FSTYPE OPTIONS /mnt/media nas01:/exports/media nfs4 rw,relatime,hard,timeo=600 Options worth knowing: hard keeps retrying when the server is unreachable, so applications wait instead of getting errors that corrupt data. soft gives up and returns an error, which can silently lose writes. File ownership uses numbers (UID and GID). If the user is 1001 on the client and 1002 on the server, permissions look wrong. Either keep IDs in sync or use a central directory. A client that cannot reach the server may freeze commands such as ls or df on that mount. Applications that hang in uninterruptible sleep (state D ) are a common sign. iSCSI iSCSI carries SCSI commands over ordinary IP. The server that offers a disk is the target , and the client is the initiator . $ sudo iscsiadm -m discovery -t sendtargets -p 10.0.1.20 10.0.1.20:3260,1 iqn.2026-01.example:storage.lun1 $ sudo iscsiadm -m node -T iqn.2026-01.example:storage.lun1 -p 10.0.1.20 --login $ lsblk | grep sdc sdc 8:32 0 200G 0 disk The new disk is raw; you create a filesystem on it. Use a separate network or VLAN for iSCSI, and multipath (two paths to the storage) so one switch failure does not remove the disk. Ceph Ceph is a distributed storage system that runs on ordinary servers. It offers block (RBD), file (CephFS) and object (RGW, S3 compatible) storage on the same cluster. OSDs store the data, one per disk. Monitors keep the cluster map and need a majority to agree. Data is split into objects placed by an algorithm called CRUSH , so no central table is needed. Each object has copies on different hosts (commonly 3), or is spread with erasure coding , which uses less space but costs CPU and repair work. $ sudo ceph -s cluster: health: HEALTH_WARN 1 osds down data: pools: 3 pools, 128 pgs usage: 4.1 TiB used, 18 TiB / 22 TiB avail pgs: 128 active+undersized+degraded A down OSD leaves data with fewer copies than intended, shown as degraded . Ceph rebuilds copies on other disks automatically. Replacing the disk quickly keeps the cluster safe, and a full cluster is a serious failure: Ceph stops writes at about 95 percent full to protect itself. Incident: The share that froze every server Incident One NFS server was rebooted, and thirty application servers hung within a minute. $ ps -eo pid, stat ,wchan:20,cmd | grep &#x27; D &#x27; 2231 D nfs_wait_bit_killable /opt/app/report-worker 2240 D nfs_wait_bit_killable df -h The applications were mounted with hard , so they waited for the server, which is the safe choice for data but blocks everything. Because even df -h touched the mount, monitoring checks hung too. The NFS server came back after 12 minutes and everything resumed. The fix was to make the exports highly available, and to stop monitoring scripts calling df on network mounts without a timeout. Common mistakes Using soft mounts for data that matters Different user IDs on client and server iSCSI on the same network as general traffic, with no multipath Letting a Ceph cluster fill past 85 percent"},"storage/performance-backups-and-troubleshooting":{"html":"\u003cp>Storage faults come in a few shapes: the disk is full, the disk is slow, the disk is gone. This lesson gives you a method for each and ends with the part that matters most: backups you have tested.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading iostat and iowait\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Finding what fills a disk, including deleted files that are still open\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Backup types, the 3-2-1 rule, and restore tests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A short troubleshooting order\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-slow-disk\">A slow disk\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"iostat -x 2 2\ntop -bn1 | grep Cpu\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>iostat -x 2 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Device   r/s    w/s   rkB/s   wkB/s  await  aqu-sz  %util\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">sda      12.0  480.0  96.0   30720.0  85.3   41.2   100.0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>top -bn1 | grep Cpu\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">%Cpu(s):  3.0 us,  2.0 sy,  0.0 ni, 12.0 id, 83.0 wa\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>await\u003c/strong> is the average wait per request in milliseconds. High await with high \u003ccode>%util\u003c/code> means the device is saturated.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>aqu-sz\u003c/strong> (queue size) shows how many requests are waiting.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>iowait\u003c/strong> (\u003ccode>wa\u003c/code>) is CPU time spent idle while waiting for disk. A CPU that is 83 percent iowait is not busy; it is waiting.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Next, find who is doing the I/O: \u003ccode>iotop\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>pidstat -d 2\u003c/code> name the process. Then decide whether to slow the writer, move it, or move to faster storage.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-full-disk\">A full disk\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"df -h /var\nsudo du -xh /var --max-depth=2 | sort -h | tail -4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span> -h /var\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Filesystem      Size  Used Avail Use% Mounted on\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/dev/sda3        50G   50G     0 100% /var\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">du\u003c/span> -xh /var --max-depth=2 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> -h | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">1.2G    /var/lib\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2.5G    /var/cache\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">4.1G    /var/log/app\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">8.0G    /var/log\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>du\u003c/code> adds up the files it can see, \u003ccode>df\u003c/code> reports what the filesystem has allocated. Here \u003ccode>du\u003c/code> finds only 8 GB of 50 GB. The difference is often \u003cstrong>a deleted file that a process still holds open\u003c/strong>. The name is gone, so \u003ccode>du\u003c/code> does not see it, but the space is not freed until the process closes it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo lsof +L1 | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> lsof +L1 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">COMMAND  PID USER  FD  TYPE  SIZE/OFF NLINK NAME\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">java    1822 app   7w  REG   40G       0     /var/log/app/old.log (deleted)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>NLINK 0\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>(deleted)\u003c/code> identify the culprit. Restarting the process frees the space. If you cannot restart it, truncating through the file descriptor (\u003ccode>: &gt; /proc/1822/fd/7\u003c/code>) frees the space without a restart, but know what you are discarding. The lasting fix is log rotation that signals the program to reopen its log.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Also check inodes with \u003ccode>df -i\u003c/code>, and file systems mounted over a directory that hides files beneath it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"backups\">Backups\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>full\u003c/strong> backup copies everything. An \u003cstrong>incremental\u003c/strong> copies changes since the last backup of any kind, and a \u003cstrong>differential\u003c/strong> copies changes since the last full one.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>3-2-1 rule\u003c/strong>: three copies, on two kinds of media, one off-site.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Snapshots\u003c/strong> are fast and local; they complement backups but do not replace them.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Immutable\u003c/strong> or write-once backups protect against ransomware that deletes the backups it can reach.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Define \u003cstrong>RPO\u003c/strong> (how much data you can lose) and \u003cstrong>RTO\u003c/strong> (how long you can be down). They set how often you back up and how fast you must restore.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>A backup you have not restored is a hope. Schedule a restore test, restore to a scratch machine, and check that the application starts and data looks right.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"troubleshooting-order\">Troubleshooting order\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Is the device present and healthy? \u003ccode>lsblk\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>dmesg\u003c/code>, SMART data.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is it mounted as expected? \u003ccode>findmnt\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>mount\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is there space and are there inodes? \u003ccode>df -h\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>df -i\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is it slow? \u003ccode>iostat -x\u003c/code>, then find the process.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is a network dependency hanging? Look for \u003ccode>D\u003c/code> state processes on NFS mounts.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-log-that-ate-the-disk\">Incident: The log that ate the disk\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A disk alert fired at 100 percent, but the team could not find the files using the space.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo du -sh /var/log\ndf -h /var/log\nsudo lsof +L1 | grep deleted\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">du\u003c/span> -sh /var/log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">3.4G    /var/log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span> -h /var/log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Filesystem      Size  Used Avail Use% Mounted on\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/dev/sda3        50G   50G     0 100% /var/log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> lsof +L1 | grep deleted\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">java  1822 app  7w  REG  44G  0  /var/log/app/app.log (deleted)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A logrotate job had removed \u003ccode>app.log\u003c/code> while the Java process kept writing to it. The 44 GB file had no name but still took the space. Restarting the service released the space and a \u003ccode>copytruncate\u003c/code> option was added to the rotation. The team had spent an hour looking for files that no longer existed by name.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Trusting \u003ccode>du\u003c/code> alone when \u003ccode>df\u003c/code> disagrees\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deleting random files to free space instead of finding the cause\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Backups with no restore test, or stored on the same disk\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading iowait as CPU load\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-slow-disk","text":"A slow disk"},{"id":"a-full-disk","text":"A full disk"},{"id":"backups","text":"Backups"},{"id":"troubleshooting-order","text":"Troubleshooting order"},{"id":"incident-the-log-that-ate-the-disk","text":"Incident: The log that ate the disk"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"iostat manual page","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/iostat.1.html"},{"t":"lsof manual page","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/lsof.8.html"},{"t":"Brendan Gregg: Linux performance","u":"https://www.brendangregg.com/linuxperf.html"}],"text":"Storage faults come in a few shapes: the disk is full, the disk is slow, the disk is gone. This lesson gives you a method for each and ends with the part that matters most: backups you have tested. What you will learn Reading iostat and iowait Finding what fills a disk, including deleted files that are still open Backup types, the 3-2-1 rule, and restore tests A short troubleshooting order A slow disk $ iostat -x 2 2 Device r/s w/s rkB/s wkB/s await aqu-sz %util sda 12.0 480.0 96.0 30720.0 85.3 41.2 100.0 $ top -bn1 | grep Cpu %Cpu(s): 3.0 us, 2.0 sy, 0.0 ni, 12.0 id, 83.0 wa await is the average wait per request in milliseconds. High await with high %util means the device is saturated. aqu-sz (queue size) shows how many requests are waiting. iowait ( wa ) is CPU time spent idle while waiting for disk. A CPU that is 83 percent iowait is not busy; it is waiting. Next, find who is doing the I/O: iotop or pidstat -d 2 name the process. Then decide whether to slow the writer, move it, or move to faster storage. A full disk $ df -h /var Filesystem Size Used Avail Use% Mounted on /dev/sda3 50G 50G 0 100% /var $ sudo du -xh /var --max-depth=2 | sort -h | tail -4 1.2G /var/lib 2.5G /var/cache 4.1G /var/log/app 8.0G /var/log du adds up the files it can see, df reports what the filesystem has allocated. Here du finds only 8 GB of 50 GB. The difference is often a deleted file that a process still holds open . The name is gone, so du does not see it, but the space is not freed until the process closes it. $ sudo lsof +L1 | head -3 COMMAND PID USER FD TYPE SIZE/OFF NLINK NAME java 1822 app 7w REG 40G 0 /var/log/app/old.log (deleted) NLINK 0 and (deleted) identify the culprit. Restarting the process frees the space. If you cannot restart it, truncating through the file descriptor ( : > /proc/1822/fd/7 ) frees the space without a restart, but know what you are discarding. The lasting fix is log rotation that signals the program to reopen its log. Also check inodes with df -i , and file systems mounted over a directory that hides files beneath it. Backups A full backup copies everything. An incremental copies changes since the last backup of any kind, and a differential copies changes since the last full one. 3-2-1 rule : three copies, on two kinds of media, one off-site. Snapshots are fast and local; they complement backups but do not replace them. Immutable or write-once backups protect against ransomware that deletes the backups it can reach. Define RPO (how much data you can lose) and RTO (how long you can be down). They set how often you back up and how fast you must restore. A backup you have not restored is a hope. Schedule a restore test, restore to a scratch machine, and check that the application starts and data looks right. Troubleshooting order Is the device present and healthy? lsblk , dmesg , SMART data. Is it mounted as expected? findmnt , mount . Is there space and are there inodes? df -h , df -i . Is it slow? iostat -x , then find the process. Is a network dependency hanging? Look for D state processes on NFS mounts. Incident: The log that ate the disk Incident A disk alert fired at 100 percent, but the team could not find the files using the space. $ sudo du -sh /var/log 3.4G /var/log $ df -h /var/log Filesystem Size Used Avail Use% Mounted on /dev/sda3 50G 50G 0 100% /var/log $ sudo lsof +L1 | grep deleted java 1822 app 7w REG 44G 0 /var/log/app/app.log (deleted) A logrotate job had removed app.log while the Java process kept writing to it. The 44 GB file had no name but still took the space. Restarting the service released the space and a copytruncate option was added to the rotation. The team had spent an hour looking for files that no longer existed by name. Common mistakes Trusting du alone when df disagrees Deleting random files to free space instead of finding the cause Backups with no restore test, or stored on the same disk Reading iowait as CPU load"},"storage/backup-and-dr-strategy-3-2-1-and-retention":{"html":"\u003cp>A backup is only useful if it lets you restore what you need, within the time you can afford, from a copy that survived the same disaster. Strategy starts from those requirements, not from the tool.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Define what to protect and how fast you must recover\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The 3-2-1 rule and its modern variations\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Full, incremental, differential and snapshot backups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retention, encryption and cost\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"start-from-objectives\">Start from objectives\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For each system write down the \u003cstrong>RPO\u003c/strong> (how much data loss is acceptable) and the \u003cstrong>RTO\u003c/strong> (how long recovery may take). A reporting database with an RPO of 24 hours can use a nightly backup. A payments database with an RPO of one minute needs continuous log shipping or replication in addition. List what must be backed up: data, but also configuration, infrastructure code, secrets, certificates, DNS zones, and the runbooks and credentials needed to restore. A restore that needs a password stored only inside the system that failed is not a plan.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-3-2-1-rule\">The 3-2-1 rule\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Keep \u003cstrong>3\u003c/strong> copies of your data (the original plus two backups), on \u003cstrong>2\u003c/strong> different kinds of media or services, with \u003cstrong>1\u003c/strong> copy off-site (another region, account or provider). Modern advice adds: \u003cstrong>1\u003c/strong> copy that is offline or immutable, and \u003cstrong>0\u003c/strong> errors after verified restores (3-2-1-1-0). The point is independence: a failure, a mistake or an attacker should not be able to reach all copies by the same path. A replica is not a backup, because it copies a deletion or corruption within seconds.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"methods\">Methods\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Full\u003c/strong>: a complete copy. Simple to restore, but large and slow.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Incremental\u003c/strong>: only changes since the last backup of any kind. Small and fast to take, but a restore needs the whole chain.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Differential\u003c/strong>: changes since the last full. Middle ground.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Snapshots\u003c/strong>: point-in-time copies in the storage layer. Very fast, but usually stored near the source, so they are not a complete answer on their own.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Deduplicating tools\u003c/strong> (restic, borg, pgBackRest, cloud backup services) store changed blocks once and make frequent backups cheap.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"restic -r s3:s3.amazonaws.com/example-backups init\nrestic -r s3:s3.amazonaws.com/example-backups backup /srv/data --tag nightly\nrestic -r s3:s3.amazonaws.com/example-backups snapshots\nrestic -r s3:s3.amazonaws.com/example-backups forget --keep-daily 7 --keep-weekly 4 --keep-monthly 12 --prune\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>restic -r s3:s3.amazonaws.com/example-backups init\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>restic -r s3:s3.amazonaws.com/example-backups backup /srv/data --tag nightly\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>restic -r s3:s3.amazonaws.com/example-backups snapshots\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>restic -r s3:s3.amazonaws.com/example-backups forget --keep-daily 7 --keep-weekly 4 --keep-monthly 12 --prune\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retention-and-cost\">Retention and cost\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Keep recent backups often and older ones rarely: for example 7 daily, 4 weekly and 12 monthly, with legal or audit requirements overriding. Longer retention helps you find slow corruption and mistakes noticed late, but it costs storage and increases the amount of personal data you hold. Encrypt backups, keep the keys separately from the data (and back up the keys safely too), restrict who can delete, and tag backups with owner and retention.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A team says &quot;we have replicas in three zones, so we don't need backups&quot;. What happens if someone runs DROP TABLE?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The deletion replicates to all copies within seconds. Replicas protect against hardware failure, not against mistakes, bugs or attacks. Backups with history do.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Replication is not backup.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-backup-in-the-same-account\">Incident: The backup in the same account\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After an administrator&#39;s credentials were stolen, the attacker deleted a production database and, with the same access, its backups.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws s3 ls s3://prod-db-backups/\naws s3api list-object-versions --bucket prod-db-backups --max-items 3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> s3://prod-db-backups/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3api list-object-versions --bucket prod-db-backups --max-items 3\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Backups lived in the same cloud account, in a bucket the same administrators could empty. The company recovered only from a six-week-old export held by a partner. Afterward it copied backups into a separate account that production credentials could not write to or delete from, enabled object versioning with a retention lock, and added alerts on backup deletion.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating replication as backup\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Storing backups where the same credentials can delete them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Backing up data but not configuration, keys and runbooks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping encryption keys only inside the system being protected\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"start-from-objectives","text":"Start from objectives"},{"id":"the-3-2-1-rule","text":"The 3-2-1 rule"},{"id":"methods","text":"Methods"},{"id":"retention-and-cost","text":"Retention and cost"},{"id":"incident-the-backup-in-the-same-account","text":"Incident: The backup in the same account"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"NIST SP 800-34: contingency planning guide","u":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/34/r1/upd1/final"},{"t":"restic documentation","u":"https://restic.readthedocs.io/en/stable/"},{"t":"Google SRE book: data integrity","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/data-integrity/"}],"text":"A backup is only useful if it lets you restore what you need, within the time you can afford, from a copy that survived the same disaster. Strategy starts from those requirements, not from the tool. What you will learn Define what to protect and how fast you must recover The 3-2-1 rule and its modern variations Full, incremental, differential and snapshot backups Retention, encryption and cost Start from objectives For each system write down the RPO (how much data loss is acceptable) and the RTO (how long recovery may take). A reporting database with an RPO of 24 hours can use a nightly backup. A payments database with an RPO of one minute needs continuous log shipping or replication in addition. List what must be backed up: data, but also configuration, infrastructure code, secrets, certificates, DNS zones, and the runbooks and credentials needed to restore. A restore that needs a password stored only inside the system that failed is not a plan. The 3-2-1 rule Keep 3 copies of your data (the original plus two backups), on 2 different kinds of media or services, with 1 copy off-site (another region, account or provider). Modern advice adds: 1 copy that is offline or immutable, and 0 errors after verified restores (3-2-1-1-0). The point is independence: a failure, a mistake or an attacker should not be able to reach all copies by the same path. A replica is not a backup, because it copies a deletion or corruption within seconds. Methods Full : a complete copy. Simple to restore, but large and slow. Incremental : only changes since the last backup of any kind. Small and fast to take, but a restore needs the whole chain. Differential : changes since the last full. Middle ground. Snapshots : point-in-time copies in the storage layer. Very fast, but usually stored near the source, so they are not a complete answer on their own. Deduplicating tools (restic, borg, pgBackRest, cloud backup services) store changed blocks once and make frequent backups cheap. $ restic -r s3:s3.amazonaws.com/example-backups init $ restic -r s3:s3.amazonaws.com/example-backups backup /srv/data --tag nightly $ restic -r s3:s3.amazonaws.com/example-backups snapshots $ restic -r s3:s3.amazonaws.com/example-backups forget --keep-daily 7 --keep-weekly 4 --keep-monthly 12 --prune Retention and cost Keep recent backups often and older ones rarely: for example 7 daily, 4 weekly and 12 monthly, with legal or audit requirements overriding. Longer retention helps you find slow corruption and mistakes noticed late, but it costs storage and increases the amount of personal data you hold. Encrypt backups, keep the keys separately from the data (and back up the keys safely too), restrict who can delete, and tag backups with owner and retention. Predict first. A team says \"we have replicas in three zones, so we don't need backups\". What happens if someone runs DROP TABLE? Your guess Answer: The deletion replicates to all copies within seconds. Replicas protect against hardware failure, not against mistakes, bugs or attacks. Backups with history do. Replication is not backup. Incident: The backup in the same account Incident After an administrator&#39;s credentials were stolen, the attacker deleted a production database and, with the same access, its backups. $ aws s3 ls s3://prod-db-backups/ $ aws s3api list-object-versions --bucket prod-db-backups --max-items 3 Backups lived in the same cloud account, in a bucket the same administrators could empty. The company recovered only from a six-week-old export held by a partner. Afterward it copied backups into a separate account that production credentials could not write to or delete from, enabled object versioning with a retention lock, and added alerts on backup deletion. Common mistakes Treating replication as backup Storing backups where the same credentials can delete them Backing up data but not configuration, keys and runbooks Keeping encryption keys only inside the system being protected"},"storage/backup-and-dr-database-backups-and-point-in-time-recovery":{"html":"\u003cp>Copying database files while the database runs does not give a usable backup. Databases need a consistent snapshot plus a record of changes, so you can go back to a moment, such as one minute before the bad delete. This lesson uses PostgreSQL for examples, and the same ideas apply to MySQL binary logs and other engines.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Logical and physical backups, and when each fits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Base backups plus continuous log archiving\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Point-in-time recovery to a chosen time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Practical tooling and what to monitor\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"logical-or-physical\">Logical or physical\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>logical backup\u003c/strong> (\u003ccode>pg_dump\u003c/code>) exports schema and data as SQL or an archive. It is portable across versions and good for single databases or small data, but restoring is slow for large data and it captures a single moment only. A \u003cstrong>physical backup\u003c/strong> copies the data files together with the write-ahead log (WAL), which is faster to restore at scale and enables point-in-time recovery.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"pg_dump -Fc -d orders -f /backup/orders-2026-03-04.dump\npg_restore -d orders_restored --jobs 4 /backup/orders-2026-03-04.dump\npg_basebackup -h db1 -U replicator -D /backup/base -Ft -z -P\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>pg_dump -Fc -d orders -f /backup/orders-2026-03-04.dump\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>pg_restore -d orders_restored --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">jobs\u003c/span> 4 /backup/orders-2026-03-04.dump\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>pg_basebackup -h db1 -U replicator -D /backup/base -Ft -z -P\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"archive-the-wal\">Archive the WAL\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every change goes to the WAL first. If you archive each WAL segment to safe storage, you can replay changes on top of a base backup up to any moment.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# postgresql.conf (shown as shell for the check): archive WAL continuously\narchive_mode=on\narchive_command='pgbackrest --stanza=main archive-push %p'\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># postgresql.conf (shown as shell for the check): archive WAL continuously\u003c/span>\narchive_mode=on\narchive_command=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;pgbackrest --stanza=main archive-push %p&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Mature tools such as \u003cstrong>pgBackRest\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>Barman\u003c/strong> manage full, differential and incremental backups, parallel transfer, compression, encryption, retention and verification, so you do not write that logic yourself. Monitor the age of the last successful backup and the lag of WAL archiving, because a quiet failure in \u003ccode>archive_command\u003c/code> makes recovery points disappear.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"recover-to-a-point-in-time\">Recover to a point in time\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"pgbackrest --stanza=main --type=time &quot;--target=2026-03-04 14:29:00+00&quot; --target-action=promote restore\npg_ctl -D /var/lib/postgresql/data start\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>pgbackrest --stanza=main --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">time\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;--target=2026-03-04 14:29:00+00&quot;\u003c/span> --target-action=promote restore\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>pg_ctl -D /var/lib/postgresql/data start\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The restore fetches the base backup, then replays WAL up to the target time and stops. Choose the target just before the mistake, restore \u003cstrong>to a new server first\u003c/strong>, check the data, and then either switch to it or copy the missing rows back. Do not restore over the damaged primary until you know what you need. Note that recovery returns the database to an earlier state, so changes made after the target are lost unless you extract them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"mysql-and-others\">MySQL and others\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>MySQL uses a full backup (for example with Percona XtraBackup) plus binary logs for point-in-time recovery. Cloud-managed databases offer automated backups and point-in-time restore with a retention window; know that window, test a restore and keep your own export for long retention and for protection against account problems.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A developer ran DELETE without a WHERE at 14:30. You restore the last nightly backup from 02:00. What is lost?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Twelve hours of valid changes. With WAL archiving you could replay up to 14:29 instead and lose almost nothing.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Continuous archiving shrinks the RPO.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-archive-that-stopped-in-january\">Incident: The archive that stopped in January\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>During a recovery drill the team found that the last archived WAL segment was nine weeks old.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"pgbackrest --stanza=main info\npsql -c &quot;select last_archived_time, last_failed_time from pg_stat_archiver&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>pgbackrest --stanza=main info\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">wal archive min/max: 000000010000000A000000F2 / 000000010000000B0000003C\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;select last_archived_time, last_failed_time from pg_stat_archiver&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 2026-01-09 03:11:42 | 2026-01-09 03:12:01\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A credential for the archive bucket had been rotated, and \u003ccode>archive_command\u003c/code> had failed ever since. The database kept running and WAL files piled up until the disk nearly filled. Nobody had alerts on archive failures. The team fixed the credential, took a new full backup, added alerts on \u003ccode>pg_stat_archiver.last_failed_time\u003c/code> and the age of the newest backup, and made a quarterly restore drill part of the calendar.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Copying live data files and calling it a backup\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No alert when archiving fails\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restoring over the damaged primary at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never testing a point-in-time restore\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"logical-or-physical","text":"Logical or physical"},{"id":"archive-the-wal","text":"Archive the WAL"},{"id":"recover-to-a-point-in-time","text":"Recover to a point in time"},{"id":"mysql-and-others","text":"MySQL and others"},{"id":"incident-the-archive-that-stopped-in-january","text":"Incident: The archive that stopped in January"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL: continuous archiving and PITR","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/continuous-archiving.html"},{"t":"pgBackRest user guide","u":"https://pgbackrest.org/user-guide.html"},{"t":"PostgreSQL: pg_dump","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/app-pgdump.html"}],"text":"Copying database files while the database runs does not give a usable backup. Databases need a consistent snapshot plus a record of changes, so you can go back to a moment, such as one minute before the bad delete. This lesson uses PostgreSQL for examples, and the same ideas apply to MySQL binary logs and other engines. What you will learn Logical and physical backups, and when each fits Base backups plus continuous log archiving Point-in-time recovery to a chosen time Practical tooling and what to monitor Logical or physical A logical backup ( pg_dump ) exports schema and data as SQL or an archive. It is portable across versions and good for single databases or small data, but restoring is slow for large data and it captures a single moment only. A physical backup copies the data files together with the write-ahead log (WAL), which is faster to restore at scale and enables point-in-time recovery. $ pg_dump -Fc -d orders -f /backup/orders-2026-03-04.dump $ pg_restore -d orders_restored -- jobs 4 /backup/orders-2026-03-04.dump $ pg_basebackup -h db1 -U replicator -D /backup/base -Ft -z -P Archive the WAL Every change goes to the WAL first. If you archive each WAL segment to safe storage, you can replay changes on top of a base backup up to any moment. # postgresql.conf (shown as shell for the check): archive WAL continuously archive_mode=on archive_command= &#x27;pgbackrest --stanza=main archive-push %p&#x27; Mature tools such as pgBackRest and Barman manage full, differential and incremental backups, parallel transfer, compression, encryption, retention and verification, so you do not write that logic yourself. Monitor the age of the last successful backup and the lag of WAL archiving, because a quiet failure in archive_command makes recovery points disappear. Recover to a point in time $ pgbackrest --stanza=main -- type = time \"--target=2026-03-04 14:29:00+00\" --target-action=promote restore $ pg_ctl -D /var/lib/postgresql/data start The restore fetches the base backup, then replays WAL up to the target time and stops. Choose the target just before the mistake, restore to a new server first , check the data, and then either switch to it or copy the missing rows back. Do not restore over the damaged primary until you know what you need. Note that recovery returns the database to an earlier state, so changes made after the target are lost unless you extract them. MySQL and others MySQL uses a full backup (for example with Percona XtraBackup) plus binary logs for point-in-time recovery. Cloud-managed databases offer automated backups and point-in-time restore with a retention window; know that window, test a restore and keep your own export for long retention and for protection against account problems. Predict first. A developer ran DELETE without a WHERE at 14:30. You restore the last nightly backup from 02:00. What is lost? Your guess Answer: Twelve hours of valid changes. With WAL archiving you could replay up to 14:29 instead and lose almost nothing. Continuous archiving shrinks the RPO. Incident: The archive that stopped in January Incident During a recovery drill the team found that the last archived WAL segment was nine weeks old. $ pgbackrest --stanza=main info wal archive min/max: 000000010000000A000000F2 / 000000010000000B0000003C $ psql -c \"select last_archived_time, last_failed_time from pg_stat_archiver\" 2026-01-09 03:11:42 | 2026-01-09 03:12:01 A credential for the archive bucket had been rotated, and archive_command had failed ever since. The database kept running and WAL files piled up until the disk nearly filled. Nobody had alerts on archive failures. The team fixed the credential, took a new full backup, added alerts on pg_stat_archiver.last_failed_time and the age of the newest backup, and made a quarterly restore drill part of the calendar. Common mistakes Copying live data files and calling it a backup No alert when archiving fails Restoring over the damaged primary at once Never testing a point-in-time re"},"storage/backup-and-dr-kubernetes-etcd-and-volume-snapshots":{"html":"\u003cp>A cluster is made of several things that fail differently: the control plane state in etcd, the manifests that describe your applications, the data in persistent volumes and the cloud resources around it. A good plan covers each of these and makes rebuilding the cluster from code a normal operation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What to protect in a cluster and in which layer\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Backing up and restoring etcd\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Volume snapshots with the CSI snapshot API\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Application-level backups and cluster rebuild strategy\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"layers-of-a-cluster\">Layers of a cluster\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Declarative config\u003c/strong>: manifests, Helm values and Terraform in version control. This is the main source for rebuilding workloads, and it is why you do not rely on etcd for application recovery in most cases.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Cluster state in etcd\u003c/strong>: needed for self-managed control planes. Managed services (EKS, AKS, GKE) run it for you, so check what the provider backs up and what you must keep yourself.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Persistent data\u003c/strong>: volumes and external databases. This is the part that cannot be recreated from code.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Secrets and keys\u003c/strong>: encryption keys, certificates and external secret stores. Back these up in a safe way that works if the cluster is gone.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2 id=\"etcd-snapshots\">etcd snapshots\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /backup/etcd-2026-03-04.db --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key\nETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /backup/etcd-2026-03-04.db --write-out=table\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /backup/etcd-2026-03-04.db --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /backup/etcd-2026-03-04.db --write-out=table\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Take snapshots on a schedule (for example every 30 minutes), copy them off the node, encrypt them (they contain Secrets), and test restoring on a throwaway cluster. Restoring etcd on a multi-node control plane needs a documented procedure: stop the API servers, restore the same snapshot on every member with a new data directory and cluster settings, then restart. Practice it before you need it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"volume-snapshots\">Volume snapshots\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The CSI snapshot API takes storage-level snapshots of PersistentVolumeClaims, if the driver supports it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: snapshot.storage.k8s.io/v1\nkind: VolumeSnapshot\nmetadata:\n  name: orders-db-2026-03-04\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  volumeSnapshotClassName: csi-snapclass\n  source:\n    persistentVolumeClaimName: orders-db-data\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">snapshot.storage.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">VolumeSnapshot\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">orders-db-2026-03-04\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">volumeSnapshotClassName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">csi-snapclass\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">source:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">persistentVolumeClaimName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">orders-db-data\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>To restore, create a new PVC with \u003ccode>dataSource\u003c/code> pointing at the snapshot. A storage snapshot is \u003cstrong>crash-consistent\u003c/strong>: it looks like the machine lost power, which databases can usually recover from but may take time. For \u003cstrong>application-consistent\u003c/strong> backups, flush or quiesce first, or use the database&#39;s own backup tool and store the result off the cluster. Snapshots usually live in the same storage system, so copy important ones to another region or account.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tools-and-rebuild-strategy\">Tools and rebuild strategy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Tools such as Velero back up namespaced resources and volumes to object storage and can restore to another cluster, which also helps migrations. A mature approach is: rebuild the cluster from infrastructure code, let your normal delivery pipeline reapply manifests from version control, then restore data volumes and databases from backups. Rehearse this end to end and measure the time.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Your cluster is destroyed. Manifests are in version control and the data is in a managed database with backups. What is the fastest safe recovery path?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Recreate the cluster from infrastructure code, redeploy manifests from version control, and restore the database from its backup. An etcd snapshot is not necessary for stateless workloads.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Rebuild from code, restore the data.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-snapshot-that-restored-a-broken-database\">Incident: The snapshot that restored a broken database\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A team restored a volume snapshot of their database after a failed upgrade, and the database then needed hours of crash recovery and lost recent transactions.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get volumesnapshot -n shop\nkubectl logs orders-db-0 | tail -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get volumesnapshot -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME                    READYTOUSE  SOURCEPVC       CREATIONTIME\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">orders-db-2026-03-04    true        orders-db-data  3h12m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs orders-db-0 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">database system was interrupted; last known up at 2026-03-04 09:47:12\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">redo starts at 0/5A0D2F8\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The snapshot was taken while the database was writing, so it was crash-consistent and recovery had to replay a long log. The team had never practiced it and had no logical or WAL-based backup as a second path. Afterward they used the database&#39;s native backups with log archiving for data, kept snapshots as a fast first option, and added a pre-snapshot hook and a restore drill each quarter.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Backing up etcd but never testing a restore\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Storing etcd snapshots unencrypted on the node\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming a volume snapshot is application-consistent\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping snapshots only in the same storage system\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"layers-of-a-cluster","text":"Layers of a cluster"},{"id":"etcd-snapshots","text":"etcd snapshots"},{"id":"volume-snapshots","text":"Volume snapshots"},{"id":"tools-and-rebuild-strategy","text":"Tools and rebuild strategy"},{"id":"incident-the-snapshot-that-restored-a-broken-database","text":"Incident: The snapshot that restored a broken database"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: operating etcd clusters","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/configure-upgrade-etcd/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: volume snapshots","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/volume-snapshots/"},{"t":"etcd: disaster recovery","u":"https://etcd.io/docs/latest/op-guide/recovery/"}],"text":"A cluster is made of several things that fail differently: the control plane state in etcd, the manifests that describe your applications, the data in persistent volumes and the cloud resources around it. A good plan covers each of these and makes rebuilding the cluster from code a normal operation. What you will learn What to protect in a cluster and in which layer Backing up and restoring etcd Volume snapshots with the CSI snapshot API Application-level backups and cluster rebuild strategy Layers of a cluster Declarative config : manifests, Helm values and Terraform in version control. This is the main source for rebuilding workloads, and it is why you do not rely on etcd for application recovery in most cases. Cluster state in etcd : needed for self-managed control planes. Managed services (EKS, AKS, GKE) run it for you, so check what the provider backs up and what you must keep yourself. Persistent data : volumes and external databases. This is the part that cannot be recreated from code. Secrets and keys : encryption keys, certificates and external secret stores. Back these up in a safe way that works if the cluster is gone. etcd snapshots $ ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /backup/etcd-2026-03-04.db --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key $ ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /backup/etcd-2026-03-04.db --write-out=table Take snapshots on a schedule (for example every 30 minutes), copy them off the node, encrypt them (they contain Secrets), and test restoring on a throwaway cluster. Restoring etcd on a multi-node control plane needs a documented procedure: stop the API servers, restore the same snapshot on every member with a new data directory and cluster settings, then restart. Practice it before you need it. Volume snapshots The CSI snapshot API takes storage-level snapshots of PersistentVolumeClaims, if the driver supports it. apiVersion: snapshot.storage.k8s.io/v1 kind: VolumeSnapshot metadata: name: orders-db-2026-03-04 namespace: shop spec: volumeSnapshotClassName: csi-snapclass source: persistentVolumeClaimName: orders-db-data To restore, create a new PVC with dataSource pointing at the snapshot. A storage snapshot is crash-consistent : it looks like the machine lost power, which databases can usually recover from but may take time. For application-consistent backups, flush or quiesce first, or use the database&#39;s own backup tool and store the result off the cluster. Snapshots usually live in the same storage system, so copy important ones to another region or account. Tools and rebuild strategy Tools such as Velero back up namespaced resources and volumes to object storage and can restore to another cluster, which also helps migrations. A mature approach is: rebuild the cluster from infrastructure code, let your normal delivery pipeline reapply manifests from version control, then restore data volumes and databases from backups. Rehearse this end to end and measure the time. Predict first. Your cluster is destroyed. Manifests are in version control and the data is in a managed database with backups. What is the fastest safe recovery path? Your guess Answer: Recreate the cluster from infrastructure code, redeploy manifests from version control, and restore the database from its backup. An etcd snapshot is not necessary for stateless workloads. Rebuild from code, restore the data. Incident: The snapshot that restored a broken database Incident A team restored a volume snapshot of their database after a failed upgrade, and the database then needed hours of crash recovery and lost recent transactions. $ kubectl get volumesnapshot -n shop NAME READYTOUSE SOURCEPVC CREATIONTIME orders-db-2026-03-04 true orders-db-data 3h12m $ kubectl logs orders-db-0 | tail -3 database system was interrupted; last known up at 2026-03-04 09:47:12 redo starts at 0/5A0D2F8 The snapshot was taken while the databa"},"storage/backup-and-dr-testing-restores-and-verifying-backups":{"html":"\u003cp>Nobody has a backup problem until they need to restore. A backup that has never been restored is a hope. The remedy is routine: restore on a schedule, check the result with real queries, record how long it took and alert when anything fails.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Three levels of verification: job success, integrity and a real restore\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Building an automated restore test\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Measuring recovery time against your RTO\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Handling backup failures like production incidents\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"three-levels-of-proof\">Three levels of proof\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>The job finished\u003c/strong>: necessary but weak. Jobs can succeed and write empty or damaged files.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Integrity checks\u003c/strong>: checksums, \u003ccode>restic check\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pgbackrest verify\u003c/code>, comparing sizes and counts with expectations.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Real restore and use\u003c/strong>: restore into a clean environment, start the system, and run application-level checks, such as row counts, the latest timestamps and a known test query.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Only the third level proves you can recover. Run it regularly, and more often for critical systems.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"restic -r s3:s3.amazonaws.com/example-backups check --read-data-subset=10%\npgbackrest --stanza=main verify\nsha256sum -c /backup/MANIFEST.sha256\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>restic -r s3:s3.amazonaws.com/example-backups check --read-data-subset=10%\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>pgbackrest --stanza=main verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sha256sum\u003c/span> -c /backup/MANIFEST.sha256\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"an-automated-restore-test\">An automated restore test\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\nstart=$(date +%s)\nworkdir=$(mktemp -d)\ntrap 'rm -rf &quot;$workdir&quot;' EXIT\n\nrestic -r &quot;$REPO&quot; restore latest --target &quot;$workdir&quot; --tag nightly\ncount=$(find &quot;$workdir&quot; -type f | wc -l)\nnewest=$(find &quot;$workdir&quot; -type f -printf '%T@\\n' | sort -n | tail -1 | cut -d. -f1)\nage=$(( $(date +%s) - newest ))\n\n[ &quot;$count&quot; -gt 1000 ] || { echo &quot;too few files: $count&quot;; exit 1; }\n[ &quot;$age&quot; -lt 172800 ] || { echo &quot;newest file is older than 48h&quot;; exit 1; }\necho &quot;restore ok: $count files, $(( $(date +%s) - start ))s&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">#!/usr/bin/env bash\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> -euo pipefail\nstart=$(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">date\u003c/span> +%s)\nworkdir=$(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">mktemp\u003c/span> -d)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">trap\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;rm -rf &quot;$workdir&quot;&#x27;\u003c/span> EXIT\n\nrestic -r \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$REPO\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> restore latest --target \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$workdir\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> --tag nightly\ncount=$(find \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$workdir\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> -\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span> f | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l)\nnewest=$(find \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$workdir\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> -\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span> f -\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">printf\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;%T@\\n&#x27;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> -n | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -1 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cut\u003c/span> -d. -f1)\nage=$(( $(date +%s) - newest ))\n\n[ \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$count\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> -gt 1000 ] || { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;too few files: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$count\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exit\u003c/span> 1; }\n[ \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$age\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> -lt 172800 ] || { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;newest file is older than 48h&quot;\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exit\u003c/span> 1; }\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;restore ok: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$count\u003c/span> files, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">$(( $(date +%s)\u003c/span> - start ))s&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Run it from a scheduler, push the duration and result to your metrics system, and alert on failure and on a restore time that grows beyond a fraction of the RTO (for example 80 percent). For databases, restore into a scratch instance and run a query set that compares counts and latest dates with production.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"timed-drills\">Timed drills\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Beyond automation, run a drill where a person follows the written runbook to restore a service in a clean environment, with a clock. This tests the human parts: access, credentials, documentation and decisions. Record steps that were unclear or missing, fix them, and repeat. Include a scenario where the person who usually does this is unavailable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when-a-backup-fails\">When a backup fails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Treat a failed or missing backup as an incident of its own priority: alert, investigate, fix and take a new backup soon, instead of waiting for the next window. Track success rate, backup age, restore time and the number of systems with a tested restore in the last quarter, and report them to the owners.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A nightly backup job exits with success every night. Can you say the data is recoverable?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> No. Success only means the job ran. Only a restore test with application-level checks proves recovery.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Verify by restoring.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-nine-months-of-empty-backups\">Incident: Nine months of empty backups\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>When a file server failed, the team discovered that the nightly backups had been empty since a path change nine months earlier.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ls -la /backup/fileserver/2026-02-28/\ngrep -c &quot;completed successfully&quot; /var/log/backup.log\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> -la /backup/fileserver/2026-02-28/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">total 8\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">drwxr-xr-x 2 backup backup 4096 Feb 28 02:10 .\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">drwxr-xr-x 9 backup backup 4096 Feb 28 02:10 ..\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;completed successfully&quot;\u003c/span> /var/log/backup.log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">271\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The job pointed at a directory that no longer existed, and the tool treated an empty source as success. Each night the log said &quot;completed successfully&quot;. The business recovered part of the data from user laptops. The team added a check on backup size against a baseline, a weekly automated restore test with file counts and a quarterly drill, and a dashboard showing the last tested restore for every system.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating &quot;job succeeded&quot; as proof\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restoring only after a disaster\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Testing restores with no application-level checks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never timing a restore against the RTO\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"three-levels-of-proof","text":"Three levels of proof"},{"id":"an-automated-restore-test","text":"An automated restore test"},{"id":"timed-drills","text":"Timed drills"},{"id":"when-a-backup-fails","text":"When a backup fails"},{"id":"incident-nine-months-of-empty-backups","text":"Incident: Nine months of empty backups"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"restic documentation","u":"https://restic.readthedocs.io/en/stable/"},{"t":"pgBackRest command reference","u":"https://pgbackrest.org/command.html"},{"t":"Google SRE book: data integrity","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/data-integrity/"}],"text":"Nobody has a backup problem until they need to restore. A backup that has never been restored is a hope. The remedy is routine: restore on a schedule, check the result with real queries, record how long it took and alert when anything fails. What you will learn Three levels of verification: job success, integrity and a real restore Building an automated restore test Measuring recovery time against your RTO Handling backup failures like production incidents Three levels of proof The job finished : necessary but weak. Jobs can succeed and write empty or damaged files. Integrity checks : checksums, restic check , pgbackrest verify , comparing sizes and counts with expectations. Real restore and use : restore into a clean environment, start the system, and run application-level checks, such as row counts, the latest timestamps and a known test query. Only the third level proves you can recover. Run it regularly, and more often for critical systems. $ restic -r s3:s3.amazonaws.com/example-backups check --read-data-subset=10% $ pgbackrest --stanza=main verify $ sha256sum -c /backup/MANIFEST.sha256 An automated restore test #!/usr/bin/env bash set -euo pipefail start=$( date +%s) workdir=$( mktemp -d) trap &#x27;rm -rf \"$workdir\"&#x27; EXIT restic -r \" $REPO \" restore latest --target \" $workdir \" --tag nightly count=$(find \" $workdir \" - type f | wc -l) newest=$(find \" $workdir \" - type f - printf &#x27;%T@\\n&#x27; | sort -n | tail -1 | cut -d. -f1) age=$(( $(date +%s) - newest )) [ \" $count \" -gt 1000 ] || { echo \"too few files: $count \" ; exit 1; } [ \" $age \" -lt 172800 ] || { echo \"newest file is older than 48h\" ; exit 1; } echo \"restore ok: $count files, $(( $(date +%s) - start ))s\" Run it from a scheduler, push the duration and result to your metrics system, and alert on failure and on a restore time that grows beyond a fraction of the RTO (for example 80 percent). For databases, restore into a scratch instance and run a query set that compares counts and latest dates with production. Timed drills Beyond automation, run a drill where a person follows the written runbook to restore a service in a clean environment, with a clock. This tests the human parts: access, credentials, documentation and decisions. Record steps that were unclear or missing, fix them, and repeat. Include a scenario where the person who usually does this is unavailable. When a backup fails Treat a failed or missing backup as an incident of its own priority: alert, investigate, fix and take a new backup soon, instead of waiting for the next window. Track success rate, backup age, restore time and the number of systems with a tested restore in the last quarter, and report them to the owners. Predict first. A nightly backup job exits with success every night. Can you say the data is recoverable? Your guess Answer: No. Success only means the job ran. Only a restore test with application-level checks proves recovery. Verify by restoring. Incident: Nine months of empty backups Incident When a file server failed, the team discovered that the nightly backups had been empty since a path change nine months earlier. $ ls -la /backup/fileserver/2026-02-28/ total 8 drwxr-xr-x 2 backup backup 4096 Feb 28 02:10 . drwxr-xr-x 9 backup backup 4096 Feb 28 02:10 .. $ grep -c \"completed successfully\" /var/log/backup.log 271 The job pointed at a directory that no longer existed, and the tool treated an empty source as success. Each night the log said \"completed successfully\". The business recovered part of the data from user laptops. The team added a check on backup size against a baseline, a weekly automated restore test with file counts and a quarterly drill, and a dashboard showing the last tested restore for every system. Common mistakes Treating \"job succeeded\" as proof Restoring only after a disaster Testing restores with no application-level checks Never timing a restore against the RTO"},"storage/backup-and-dr-disaster-recovery-patterns-and-failover":{"html":"\u003cp>Disaster recovery (DR) is the ability to run your service somewhere else after losing a site, a region or an account. The right design depends on how much downtime and data loss the business accepts and how much it will pay. This lesson compares the standard patterns and the details that decide whether they work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The four common DR patterns and their trade-offs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Replicating data, and why order and consistency matter\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Traffic failover with DNS and load balancers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Failback and keeping the DR site honest\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"four-patterns\">Four patterns\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Backup and restore\u003c/strong>: copies of data and the code to rebuild live elsewhere. Cheapest, with an RTO of hours to days.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Pilot light\u003c/strong>: the core data layer runs and replicates continuously in the DR site, while application servers are off or minimal and are started on demand. RTO is tens of minutes to hours.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Warm standby\u003c/strong>: a smaller but complete copy runs all the time and scales up on failover. RTO is minutes.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Active-active\u003c/strong> (multi-site): several sites serve live traffic. RTO near zero, with the highest cost and the hardest data consistency problems.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Pick per service. A payment system may justify warm standby or active-active. An internal wiki can use backup and restore.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"data-is-the-hard-part\">Data is the hard part\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Stateless servers are easy to rebuild. Data needs replication: asynchronous replication keeps latency low but can lose the last seconds on failover (non-zero RPO), while synchronous replication across distance adds latency to every write and can reduce availability. Decide RPO per data store, and ensure related data fails over together (a database and its object storage, a queue and its consumers), or you will see orders without their files. Think about secrets, certificates, container images and configuration, which must exist in the DR site before the disaster, not be fetched from the lost site.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"moving-traffic\">Moving traffic\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"dig +short app.example.com\naws route53 list-health-checks --max-items 3\ncurl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\\n' https://dr.example.com/health\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dig +short app.example.com\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws route53 list-health-checks --max-items 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s -o /dev/null -w \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;%{http_code}\\n&#x27;\u003c/span> https://dr.example.com/health\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Common methods: DNS failover with health checks and a short TTL (some clients cache longer than the TTL), global load balancers or anycast addresses (faster, provider specific), and manual switching steps in a runbook. Decide who is allowed to trigger failover and with which evidence, since a false failover causes its own outage and a split-brain risk if both sites accept writes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"failback-and-keeping-dr-honest\">Failback and keeping DR honest\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>After the primary returns, plan the way back: re-sync data in the reverse direction, switch traffic in a quiet window and then re-establish the original protection. Keep the DR site tested and in step with production: run infrastructure from the same code, deploy to both, use game days to fail over real traffic for non-critical services, and compare capacity so the DR site can carry the load. A DR site that is a year behind production will fail when you need it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You use asynchronous replication to another region and the primary fails with 5 seconds of replication lag. What is the cost of failing over?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Up to roughly 5 seconds of recent writes may be lost. That is the RPO of asynchronous replication, which must be acceptable to the business.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Async replication means non-zero RPO.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-standby-that-could-not-take-the-load\">Incident: The standby that could not take the load\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>When a region failed, the standby in the second region started but ran out of capacity within minutes and returned errors.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get nodes --context dr-cluster | wc -l\nkubectl get nodes --context prod-cluster | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get nodes --context dr-cluster | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get nodes --context prod-cluster | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">36\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The DR cluster had been sized for &quot;a small copy&quot; and had a quota limit on instance launches that nobody had raised. Autoscaling hit the quota after three nodes. Failover succeeded technically but the service was degraded for 90 minutes. The team raised quotas, pre-provisioned capacity for the critical tier, ran a load test on the DR site each quarter and added the DR capacity to the architecture review.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choosing a pattern without agreed RTO and RPO\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Replicating data but not secrets, images and configuration\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No decision rule for who triggers failover\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A DR site that drifts from production or lacks capacity and quota\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"four-patterns","text":"Four patterns"},{"id":"data-is-the-hard-part","text":"Data is the hard part"},{"id":"moving-traffic","text":"Moving traffic"},{"id":"failback-and-keeping-dr-honest","text":"Failback and keeping DR honest"},{"id":"incident-the-standby-that-could-not-take-the-load","text":"Incident: The standby that could not take the load"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS: disaster recovery options in the cloud","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/disaster-recovery-workloads-on-aws/disaster-recovery-options-in-the-cloud.html"},{"t":"Azure Well-Architected: disaster recovery","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/well-architected/reliability/disaster-recovery"},{"t":"NIST SP 800-34: contingency planning guide","u":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/34/r1/upd1/final"}],"text":"Disaster recovery (DR) is the ability to run your service somewhere else after losing a site, a region or an account. The right design depends on how much downtime and data loss the business accepts and how much it will pay. This lesson compares the standard patterns and the details that decide whether they work. What you will learn The four common DR patterns and their trade-offs Replicating data, and why order and consistency matter Traffic failover with DNS and load balancers Failback and keeping the DR site honest Four patterns Backup and restore : copies of data and the code to rebuild live elsewhere. Cheapest, with an RTO of hours to days. Pilot light : the core data layer runs and replicates continuously in the DR site, while application servers are off or minimal and are started on demand. RTO is tens of minutes to hours. Warm standby : a smaller but complete copy runs all the time and scales up on failover. RTO is minutes. Active-active (multi-site): several sites serve live traffic. RTO near zero, with the highest cost and the hardest data consistency problems. Pick per service. A payment system may justify warm standby or active-active. An internal wiki can use backup and restore. Data is the hard part Stateless servers are easy to rebuild. Data needs replication: asynchronous replication keeps latency low but can lose the last seconds on failover (non-zero RPO), while synchronous replication across distance adds latency to every write and can reduce availability. Decide RPO per data store, and ensure related data fails over together (a database and its object storage, a queue and its consumers), or you will see orders without their files. Think about secrets, certificates, container images and configuration, which must exist in the DR site before the disaster, not be fetched from the lost site. Moving traffic $ dig +short app.example.com $ aws route53 list-health-checks --max-items 3 $ curl -s -o /dev/null -w &#x27;%{http_code}\\n&#x27; https://dr.example.com/health Common methods: DNS failover with health checks and a short TTL (some clients cache longer than the TTL), global load balancers or anycast addresses (faster, provider specific), and manual switching steps in a runbook. Decide who is allowed to trigger failover and with which evidence, since a false failover causes its own outage and a split-brain risk if both sites accept writes. Failback and keeping DR honest After the primary returns, plan the way back: re-sync data in the reverse direction, switch traffic in a quiet window and then re-establish the original protection. Keep the DR site tested and in step with production: run infrastructure from the same code, deploy to both, use game days to fail over real traffic for non-critical services, and compare capacity so the DR site can carry the load. A DR site that is a year behind production will fail when you need it. Predict first. You use asynchronous replication to another region and the primary fails with 5 seconds of replication lag. What is the cost of failing over? Your guess Answer: Up to roughly 5 seconds of recent writes may be lost. That is the RPO of asynchronous replication, which must be acceptable to the business. Async replication means non-zero RPO. Incident: The standby that could not take the load Incident When a region failed, the standby in the second region started but ran out of capacity within minutes and returned errors. $ kubectl get nodes --context dr-cluster | wc -l 4 $ kubectl get nodes --context prod-cluster | wc -l 36 The DR cluster had been sized for \"a small copy\" and had a quota limit on instance launches that nobody had raised. Autoscaling hit the quota after three nodes. Failover succeeded technically but the service was degraded for 90 minutes. The team raised quotas, pre-provisioned capacity for the critical tier, ran a load test on the DR site each quarter and added the DR capacity to the architecture review. Common mistakes Choosing a pattern without agreed RTO and"},"storage/backup-and-dr-ransomware-immutable-backups-and-runbooks":{"html":"\u003cp>Attackers know that backups are the last line of defense, so they try to find and destroy them first. A modern backup design assumes that an administrator account may be compromised, and keeps at least one copy that nobody can change or delete during its retention period. Preparing the recovery runbook before the event matters just as much as the technology.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How attacks target backups and what stops them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Immutability, object lock and air gaps\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Separate accounts, least privilege and monitoring\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A recovery runbook and an order of restoration\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-attackers-do\">What attackers do\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A typical attack gains credentials, spreads, steals data, deletes or encrypts backups and then encrypts production. They look for backup consoles, backup servers joined to the same directory, storage buckets with write and delete rights, and snapshots in the same account. Defenses must therefore break the link between &quot;can reach production&quot; and &quot;can destroy backups&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"immutable-and-isolated-copies\">Immutable and isolated copies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Immutability / object lock / WORM\u003c/strong>: the storage refuses deletes and overwrites until a retention date, even for administrators in the retention-lock mode. Cloud object stores, backup appliances and tape libraries support this.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Separate account or provider\u003c/strong>: backups live in an account with different credentials and a separate identity, reached only by a narrow write-only role from production.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Air gap\u003c/strong>: offline media or a logically isolated vault that is not reachable during normal operations.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Versioning plus MFA delete\u003c/strong> and long enough retention that you can go back before the attacker arrived. Attackers often wait weeks.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws s3api put-bucket-versioning --bucket dr-vault-backups --versioning-configuration Status=Enabled\naws s3api put-object-lock-configuration --bucket dr-vault-backups --object-lock-configuration '{&quot;ObjectLockEnabled&quot;:&quot;Enabled&quot;,&quot;Rule&quot;:{&quot;DefaultRetention&quot;:{&quot;Mode&quot;:&quot;COMPLIANCE&quot;,&quot;Days&quot;:35}}}'\naws s3api get-object-lock-configuration --bucket dr-vault-backups\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3api put-bucket-versioning --bucket dr-vault-backups --versioning-configuration Status=Enabled\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3api put-object-lock-configuration --bucket dr-vault-backups --object-lock-configuration \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{&quot;ObjectLockEnabled&quot;:&quot;Enabled&quot;,&quot;Rule&quot;:{&quot;DefaultRetention&quot;:{&quot;Mode&quot;:&quot;COMPLIANCE&quot;,&quot;Days&quot;:35}}}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3api get-object-lock-configuration --bucket dr-vault-backups\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Object lock is normally enabled when the bucket is created, and compliance mode cannot be shortened by anyone, so choose retention carefully. Alert on any delete, policy change or failed backup, and keep the backup system&#39;s own admin accounts under separate, strongly authenticated control.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-recovery-runbook\">The recovery runbook\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write it before you need it, store it where it is reachable when systems are down (printed copy, offline copy, separate system), and include:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Who decides and who does\u003c/strong>: incident lead, communications lead and technical leads with phone numbers that do not depend on company systems.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Contain first\u003c/strong>: isolate affected systems and accounts, rotate credentials, and preserve evidence for investigation. Restoring into an environment that is still compromised wastes the restore.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Order of restoration\u003c/strong>: identity and DNS, networking, secrets and certificates, data stores, then applications, by dependency and business priority.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Clean restore points\u003c/strong>: how to choose a backup from before the compromise, and how to scan restored data.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Verification and sign-off\u003c/strong>: checks that each service is correct before it takes traffic.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Your backups sit in a bucket that the production admin role can delete from. What is the weakness?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> A compromised admin can delete production and backups together. Use a separate account with a write-only path and object lock so nobody can delete within retention.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Separate the ability to write from the ability to delete.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-restoring-into-a-compromised-network\">Incident: Restoring into a compromised network\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a ransomware attack, a company restored its servers from backup within a day, and they were encrypted again two hours later.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"last -n 3\ncrontab -l -u svc-backup\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>last -n 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">admin pts/0 203.0.113.55 Mar 4 01:12 still logged in\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>crontab -l -u svc-backup\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">*/5 * * * * /tmp/.cache/agent\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The attacker&#39;s persistence (a stolen service account and a scheduled task) was still in place. Backups were good, but the environment was not clean. The company rebuilt identity and rotated all credentials first, restored into a new network segment from a point before the first compromise, scanned the data and brought services back in dependency order. Their new runbook starts with containment and has checklists for each step.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Backups reachable with the same credentials as production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retention shorter than the time attackers stay hidden\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restoring before containing the intrusion\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A runbook that exists only on systems that are down\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-attackers-do","text":"What attackers do"},{"id":"immutable-and-isolated-copies","text":"Immutable and isolated copies"},{"id":"the-recovery-runbook","text":"The recovery runbook"},{"id":"incident-restoring-into-a-compromised-network","text":"Incident: Restoring into a compromised network"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Amazon S3 Object Lock","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lock.html"},{"t":"CISA: stop ransomware","u":"https://www.cisa.gov/stopransomware"},{"t":"NIST SP 1800-25: identifying and protecting assets against ransomware","u":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/1800/25/final"}],"text":"Attackers know that backups are the last line of defense, so they try to find and destroy them first. A modern backup design assumes that an administrator account may be compromised, and keeps at least one copy that nobody can change or delete during its retention period. Preparing the recovery runbook before the event matters just as much as the technology. What you will learn How attacks target backups and what stops them Immutability, object lock and air gaps Separate accounts, least privilege and monitoring A recovery runbook and an order of restoration What attackers do A typical attack gains credentials, spreads, steals data, deletes or encrypts backups and then encrypts production. They look for backup consoles, backup servers joined to the same directory, storage buckets with write and delete rights, and snapshots in the same account. Defenses must therefore break the link between \"can reach production\" and \"can destroy backups\". Immutable and isolated copies Immutability / object lock / WORM : the storage refuses deletes and overwrites until a retention date, even for administrators in the retention-lock mode. Cloud object stores, backup appliances and tape libraries support this. Separate account or provider : backups live in an account with different credentials and a separate identity, reached only by a narrow write-only role from production. Air gap : offline media or a logically isolated vault that is not reachable during normal operations. Versioning plus MFA delete and long enough retention that you can go back before the attacker arrived. Attackers often wait weeks. $ aws s3api put-bucket-versioning --bucket dr-vault-backups --versioning-configuration Status=Enabled $ aws s3api put-object-lock-configuration --bucket dr-vault-backups --object-lock-configuration &#x27;{\"ObjectLockEnabled\":\"Enabled\",\"Rule\":{\"DefaultRetention\":{\"Mode\":\"COMPLIANCE\",\"Days\":35}}}&#x27; $ aws s3api get-object-lock-configuration --bucket dr-vault-backups Object lock is normally enabled when the bucket is created, and compliance mode cannot be shortened by anyone, so choose retention carefully. Alert on any delete, policy change or failed backup, and keep the backup system&#39;s own admin accounts under separate, strongly authenticated control. The recovery runbook Write it before you need it, store it where it is reachable when systems are down (printed copy, offline copy, separate system), and include: Who decides and who does : incident lead, communications lead and technical leads with phone numbers that do not depend on company systems. Contain first : isolate affected systems and accounts, rotate credentials, and preserve evidence for investigation. Restoring into an environment that is still compromised wastes the restore. Order of restoration : identity and DNS, networking, secrets and certificates, data stores, then applications, by dependency and business priority. Clean restore points : how to choose a backup from before the compromise, and how to scan restored data. Verification and sign-off : checks that each service is correct before it takes traffic. Predict first. Your backups sit in a bucket that the production admin role can delete from. What is the weakness? Your guess Answer: A compromised admin can delete production and backups together. Use a separate account with a write-only path and object lock so nobody can delete within retention. Separate the ability to write from the ability to delete. Incident: Restoring into a compromised network Incident After a ransomware attack, a company restored its servers from backup within a day, and they were encrypted again two hours later. $ last -n 3 admin pts/0 203.0.113.55 Mar 4 01:12 still logged in $ crontab -l -u svc-backup */5 * * * * /tmp/.cache/agent The attacker&#39;s persistence (a stolen service account and a scheduled task) was still in place. Backups were good, but the environment was not clean. The company rebuilt identity and rotated all credentials first, restor"},"storage/storage-benchmarking-with-fio":{"html":"\u003cp>Disks are described by throughput numbers that rarely match application behavior. A database does small random reads and writes, while backups stream large files. fio lets you reproduce both and see the difference.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What IOPS, bandwidth and latency mean\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing fio jobs for common workloads\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading percentile latency\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Safe testing practices\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-basics\">The basics\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>IOPS\u003c/strong> counts operations per second, \u003cstrong>bandwidth\u003c/strong> is bytes per second and \u003cstrong>latency\u003c/strong> is the time per operation. They depend on block size and queue depth. Small random operations stress IOPS and latency, while large sequential operations stress bandwidth.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"jobs-for-real-workloads\">Jobs for real workloads\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"; db-like.fio  - random 8k, 70% reads, queue depth 32\n[db-like]\nfilename=/mnt/scratch/fio.dat\nsize=4G\ndirect=1\nioengine=libaio\nrw=randrw\nrwmixread=70\nbs=8k\niodepth=32\nnumjobs=4\ntime_based=1\nruntime=60\ngroup_reporting=1\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">; db-like.fio  - random 8k, 70% reads, queue depth 32\n[db-like]\nfilename=/mnt/scratch/fio.dat\nsize=4G\ndirect=1\nioengine=libaio\nrw=randrw\nrwmixread=70\nbs=8k\niodepth=32\nnumjobs=4\ntime_based=1\nruntime=60\ngroup_reporting=1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"fio db-like.fio\nfio --name=seqread --filename=/mnt/scratch/fio.dat --size=4G --direct=1 \\\n    --rw=read --bs=1M --iodepth=8 --runtime=30 --time_based\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">fio db-like.fio\nfio --name=seqread --filename=/mnt/scratch/fio.dat --size=4G --direct=1 \\\n    --rw=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">read\u003c/span> --bs=1M --iodepth=8 --runtime=30 --time_based\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>direct=1\u003c/code> bypasses the page cache, otherwise you measure memory. Match \u003ccode>bs\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>iodepth\u003c/code> and the read-write mix to your application. Run longer than the time a burst allowance lasts on cloud volumes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-the-result\">Reading the result\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">read: IOPS=11.2k, BW=87.5MiB/s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  clat percentiles (usec):\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">   | 50.00th=[  620], 95.00th=[ 1860], 99.00th=[ 7300], 99.90th=[21100]\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Look at the \u003cstrong>percentiles\u003c/strong>, especially the 99th and 99.9th. Averages hide the slow tail that users feel. Compare results with the limits of the volume type, and watch \u003ccode>iostat -x\u003c/code> during the test.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"safety\">Safety\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Never run write tests on a device holding data. Use a scratch file or a new volume, and delete it afterwards. Test during maintenance windows on shared storage, since heavy tests affect neighbours.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-fast-in-the-test-slow-in-production\">Incident: Fast in the test, slow in production\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A sequential test hid poor random latency.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">seqread: BW=1.2GiB/s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">randread 4k, iodepth=1: p99 latency 14ms\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">database commit latency: 19ms\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The team had chosen a throughput-optimized volume after a large-file test. The database needed low-latency random I/O. They switched to a volume type designed for IOPS and confirmed it with a random test that matched the database settings.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Testing without direct I/O\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Only measuring sequential throughput\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading averages and ignoring the tail\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing to a device that holds data\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-basics","text":"The basics"},{"id":"jobs-for-real-workloads","text":"Jobs for real workloads"},{"id":"reading-the-result","text":"Reading the result"},{"id":"safety","text":"Safety"},{"id":"incident-fast-in-the-test-slow-in-production","text":"Incident: Fast in the test, slow in production"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"fio documentation","u":"https://fio.readthedocs.io/en/latest/"},{"t":"Linux block layer documentation","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/block/index.html"}],"text":"Disks are described by throughput numbers that rarely match application behavior. A database does small random reads and writes, while backups stream large files. fio lets you reproduce both and see the difference. What you will learn What IOPS, bandwidth and latency mean Writing fio jobs for common workloads Reading percentile latency Safe testing practices The basics IOPS counts operations per second, bandwidth is bytes per second and latency is the time per operation. They depend on block size and queue depth. Small random operations stress IOPS and latency, while large sequential operations stress bandwidth. Jobs for real workloads ; db-like.fio - random 8k, 70% reads, queue depth 32 [db-like] filename=/mnt/scratch/fio.dat size=4G direct=1 ioengine=libaio rw=randrw rwmixread=70 bs=8k iodepth=32 numjobs=4 time_based=1 runtime=60 group_reporting=1 fio db-like.fio fio --name=seqread --filename=/mnt/scratch/fio.dat --size=4G --direct=1 \\ --rw= read --bs=1M --iodepth=8 --runtime=30 --time_based direct=1 bypasses the page cache, otherwise you measure memory. Match bs , iodepth and the read-write mix to your application. Run longer than the time a burst allowance lasts on cloud volumes. Reading the result read: IOPS=11.2k, BW=87.5MiB/s clat percentiles (usec): | 50.00th=[ 620], 95.00th=[ 1860], 99.00th=[ 7300], 99.90th=[21100] Look at the percentiles , especially the 99th and 99.9th. Averages hide the slow tail that users feel. Compare results with the limits of the volume type, and watch iostat -x during the test. Safety Never run write tests on a device holding data. Use a scratch file or a new volume, and delete it afterwards. Test during maintenance windows on shared storage, since heavy tests affect neighbours. Incident: Fast in the test, slow in production Incident A sequential test hid poor random latency. seqread: BW=1.2GiB/s randread 4k, iodepth=1: p99 latency 14ms database commit latency: 19ms The team had chosen a throughput-optimized volume after a large-file test. The database needed low-latency random I/O. They switched to a volume type designed for IOPS and confirmed it with a random test that matched the database settings. Common mistakes Testing without direct I/O Only measuring sequential throughput Reading averages and ignoring the tail Writing to a device that holds data"}});